<!-- =======================================================
     02 / STRATEGY
     ======================================================= -->

<CaseSection
  number="02"
  label="Strategy"
  title="Simplifying the journey before redesigning the interface."
>
  <p>
    I worked as the project's only UI/UX designer,
    responsible for the overall visual direction,
    information architecture, page flow, layouts,
    component decisions, and image selection across
    the new website.
  </p>

  <h3>
    Reducing unnecessary paths
  </h3>

  <p>
    One of the largest problems was structural rather
    than visual. The previous website contained many
    overlapping, redundant, or low-priority pages.
  </p>

  <p>
    Instead of carrying that complete structure into
    a new interface, I reorganized the information
    architecture and consolidated related content
    into clearer destinations.
  </p>

  <h3>
    Designing within a real team
  </h3>

  <p>
    Development was handled by a two-person team. I
    contributed mainly to frontend implementation,
    while another developer focused more heavily on
    backend functionality and custom development.
  </p>

  <p>
    The project was supervised by a lecturer acting
    as both team leader and stakeholder, who reviewed
    and approved the design direction throughout the
    process.
  </p>
</CaseSection>


<!-- =======================================================
     03 / DESIGN DIRECTION
     ======================================================= -->

<CaseSection
  number="03"
  label="Design Direction"
  title="Building a contemporary editorial system while preserving UGM's institutional identity."
>
  <p>
    Early in the project, I studied university
    websites whose structure and editorial treatment
    aligned with the experience we wanted to create.
  </p>

  <p>
    A Japanese university website became the primary
    visual reference for the agreed direction. The
    qualities we liked were translated into a system
    appropriate for FMIPA while preserving UGM's
    existing identity requirements, including its
    colors and institutional marks.
  </p>

  <p>
    Beyond those brand constraints, I had significant
    flexibility in defining typography, spacing,
    composition, imagery, navigation, and reusable
    interface components.
  </p>
</CaseSection>


