<!-- =======================================================
     06 / REFLECTION
     ======================================================= -->

<CaseSection
  number="06"
  label="Reflection"
  title="The redesign became an exercise in balancing design, content, maintainability, and implementation."
>
  <p>
    The project gave me responsibility over a much
    broader design problem than an individual screen
    or isolated feature.
  </p>

  <p>
    Decisions about navigation, content hierarchy,
    reusable components, imagery, and visual
    direction needed to work across an entire
    institutional website.
  </p>

  <p>
    Working alongside implementation also made the
    trade-offs more tangible. A design could not only
    look appropriate in Figma; it also needed to work
    within WordPress, existing infrastructure, free
    tooling, and the resources available to the team.
  </p>

  <p>
    That combination strengthened the way I approach
    UI/UX design: not as a visual layer applied before
    development, but as part of a larger system that
    must remain useful and maintainable after the
    design file is finished.
  </p>
</CaseSection>